From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_STAGING_STEPS=1: staging buffers over 8 MiB in 8 MiB steps, idle ones let go each frame beyond 64 MiB (lethevimlet/oot-bc250 eeea507); EDEN_UP_STAGING_KEEP=1: one 128 MiB upload staging buffer made at the start and kept; both off by default

lethevimlet/oot-bc250 eeea507 (Ocarina of Time on the BC-250, same GPU family and driver):
staging buffers were powers of two and were let go one size level per frame. A Kokiri Forest
load held 1.1 GiB of 256 MiB staging buffers while the board swapped the game out (reported
~70 MiB held after the change). Ours rounds the same way (CreateStagingBuffer,
`.size = 1ULL << log2_size`). On the console prepare-vulkan-port.py already makes a buffer the
CPU maps its transfer in 16 MiB steps (headless/staging_buffers.h). What is new there: 8 MiB
steps, the same for buffers only the GPU uses (the BC transcode output and the compute passes,
still powers of two: a 136 MiB transcode output takes 256 MiB), and the prompt release.

EDEN_UP_STAGING_STEPS=1 (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"):
- a staging buffer over 8 MiB is its transfer rounded up to 8 MiB, never more than the size
  chosen before (on the console the smaller of its 16 MiB step and the 8 MiB step);
- each buffer records its size (capacity), and a kept one is handed out only when it holds the
  transfer;
- before a new buffer over 8 MiB is made, the idle ones over 8 MiB of its kind are let go;
- each TickFrame, idle buffers over 8 MiB beyond 64 MiB per kind (device-local, upload,
  download) are let go, the largest kept first. With EDEN_UP_STAGING_REUSE on, this limits the
  idle 16 and 32 MiB upload buffers it keeps to 64 MiB, and making any new upload buffer over
  8 MiB lets them all go first.
Upstream's opt-out (EDEN_NX2_STAGING_POW2) is turned into this opt-in switch.
On the console it gains little for Ocarina: device-local staging is about 0.6 MiB at the door
and CPU-mapped buffers already come in 16 MiB steps. Its let-go-before-making can drop idle
buffers in the middle of a burst that the next request would have reused, which can add fresh
allocations in the door frame. Test it on its own, not stacked with KEEP. Same as upstream: the
let-go-before-making can also drop a download buffer the GPU is done with before the CPU has
read it; no caller asks for staging between Finish and that read today.

EDEN_UP_STAGING_KEEP=1 (not upstream): the door hitch (batch t87-a, runs 739-743). In the ~1 s
GPU-thread frame as the village appears, the buffer cache asks the pool for 128 MiB and then
64 MiB of upload staging with nothing that large in it (stderr MKW_STAGING_MISS, upload pool
busy=3 free=0): each a fresh allocation and mapping. With KEEP one upload buffer is made at the
start, 128 MiB (EDEN_UP_STAGING_KEEP_MIB=<16..1024> sets it, rounded up to a power of two),
written once and never swept. Only TakeKeptUpload hands it out, the normal lookup skips it: an
upload that is not deferred, over one ring region and over a quarter of its size, takes it when
no other buffer of its own level is free and the GPU is done with the kept one. The 128 MiB
upload takes it, and the 64 MiB one too when the GPU has finished the first copy (in the run
checked it had: tick 8296, completed 8288, the first copy 8268). 128 MiB is the size that covers both: at 256 MiB or more the 64 MiB upload is not
over a quarter and makes its own buffer, at 64 MiB or less the 128 MiB one does not fit. The
guest read into it (ReadGuestForBuffer) costs what it did.
What is saved for sure is the allocation and mapping. Whether the first write costs anything on
the console is not shown: the graphics pool may not fault on first touch. 0498's "staging made"
time ends once the buffer is made and mapped; a first-write cost would show in its "buffer
upload: 128.0 MiB ... took X ms" line, so compare that line, KEEP against control, with
EDEN_UP_HITCH_TRACE=1.
On the console ReleaseCompleted (memory short, a refused buffer) lets it go like any idle
buffer; it is not made again, and the next large upload logs that. In the t87-a runs memory is
not short before the door (short=0) and goes short right after it, so the kept buffer reaches
the door but later areas get nothing from it. Making it at the start prints one set of
MKW_STAGING_MISS lines (asked=134217728 at the default size, before the first frame): door-miss
counts skip it.
KEEP_MIB without KEEP=1 only logs a warning.

Log lines (eden_log): "EDEN_UP_STAGING_STEPS: N MiB staging buffer for B bytes, not P MiB"
(1st, 2nd, 4th ...), "EDEN_UP_STAGING_KEEP: 128 MiB upload staging buffer made at the start and
kept", "... used for B bytes, n times so far" (every use counted, 1st, 2nd, 4th ... logged),
"... was let go".

Unset: buffers are sized, handed out and swept as before. The new capacity field is filled but
read only under STEPS or by the kept buffer; kept is never set.

PS5 prepare-vulkan-port.py: every anchor in these two files is byte-identical and still matches
once (CreateStagingBuffer's signature, the vulkan_wrapper.h include, TickFrame's signature, the
is_free lambda, the log2_size line, `.size = 1ULL << log2_size,`, the CreateBuffer line, the
header's TickFrame declaration). The step is applied after the create info is built, so it
takes the smaller of it and the console's `made`; the console's OOM retry only shrinks it
further and capacity is read after it. Known gap: staging_buffers.h Holds() takes a GPU-only
buffer to be its power of two, so under STEPS the "MiB of finished staging" the refusal hook
counts is too high for those (by under half each), which can skip the guest-reserve lend in a
refusal. Negligible in Ocarina; Holds() reading capacity would fix it.

Not compiled or run here. git apply --check: clean on T88 and on T88 plus 0498-hitch-trace, in
either order.

